/*!
    \example interfaceframework/smartplug
    \meta tag {ifcodegen, qface-annotations, mqtt}
    \title Qt Interface Framework - Smart Plug (MQTT)
    \ingroup qtinterfaceframework-examples
    \brief Controlling a smart plug over MQTT and selecting the broker at runtime.

    \image smartplug.png

    This example shows how to use the \l{QtInterfaceFramework}{Qt Interface Framework}
    with the \l{backend-mqtt-template}{backend_mqtt} template to talk to a smart plug over
    MQTT. The application can switch the plug on and off and displays its power metering
    (voltage, current and accumulated energy). It also lets the user choose which MQTT
    broker to connect to at runtime.

    \section1 The interface

    The plug is described by a single QFace interface. The writable \c on property switches
    the relay, while \c current, \c voltage and \c energy are read-only measurements. The
    \c config_mqtt annotation maps every property to a topic below the \c smartplug prefix
    (for example \c smartplug/on) and marks them optional, so the backend reports
    itself as initialized as soon as it is connected - even before the device has published
    anything.

    \quotefile interfaceframework/smartplug/example-smartplug.qface

    From this interface the \l{ifcodegen}{ifcodegen} generates a QML frontend module and,
    with the \c backend_mqtt template, an MQTT backend plugin:

    \quotefromfile interfaceframework/smartplug/CMakeLists.txt
    \skipto qt_ifcodegen_add_qml_module
    \printuntil )
    \skipto qt_ifcodegen_add_plugin
    \printuntil )

    \section1 Using the plug in QML

    The generated \c SmartPlug type is used like any other Interface Framework feature.
    Binding to its properties drives the UI, and assigning to \c on publishes the command:

    \snippet interfaceframework/smartplug/Main.qml feature

    \section1 Choosing the broker

    The broker is not hard-coded. An \l InterfaceFrameworkConfiguration item provides the
    \c connectionUrl service setting for the module; changing it at runtime makes the MQTT
    backend reconnect to the new broker:

    \snippet interfaceframework/smartplug/Main.qml configuration

    The \c BrokerBar component lets the user pick a preset or type a custom URL and feeds
    the result back into that configuration.

    \section1 Running the example

    The application is only the MQTT \e client. To see it do something you need a broker and
    a device publishing the plug's data:

    \list 1
        \li Start an MQTT broker on \c localhost, for example \c mosquitto.
        \li Start the bundled \c smartplug-simulation helper. It simulates a real plug: it
            reacts to \c smartplug/on and publishes changing \c smartplug/current,
            \c smartplug/voltage and \c smartplug/energy values. Pass \c --url to point it at
            a different broker (\c --help lists the options).
        \li Start the \c smartplug application. Toggle the switch and watch the metering
            update.
    \endlist
*/
